△▼△▼△▼△

"I've been a real burden on you, old man. In every way possible."

Aldebaran's apology came after he'd mercilessly beaten down Heinckel for nearly acting rashly during his conversation with Felt.

Honestly, Heinckel knew he'd almost crossed a line he couldn't come back from. He had no right to complain about being punched. There was no choice but to accept the apology.

Except for one thing—the fact that Felt had noticed. That one truth absolutely couldn't be left alone.

Luanna Astrea, Heinckel's wife, who slept endlessly.

The fact that her slumber was caused by their son, Reinhard.

"Hold up. I ain't asking what went down between you and Felt-chan. But like I said—I'm sorry for dumping everything on you, old man."

"...I don't follow. What good does apologizing do?"

"Obviously, I ain't saying you gotta keep putting up with it. For now, we'll handle Felt-chan."

"Handle her how...?"

"Won't do anything violent. Might not be gentle, though."

Heinckel's face hardened at Aldebaran's shrug. The man was inscrutable, but one thing was clear—his grief over losing Priscilla had driven him to make enemies of the world.

At his core, Aldebaran truly had loved that formidable "Sun Princess."

Yet he still retained just enough conscience to stop short of sacrificing everything. Or at least, Heinckel had thought so—until now, when it felt like that restraint had finally snapped.

"What... are you planning?"

"Won't screw you over, old man. Just need to confirm one thing."

Heinckel's throat clicked audibly, a sudden dryness overwhelming him. He wanted—and didn't want—to hear what came next. The coward's intuition.

Unaware of Heinckel's turmoil, Aldebaran asked his question.

It was—

"About the name of that royal who got kidnapped from the castle fifteen years ago—"

To speak that name was taboo—not just for Heinckel, but for the entire Lugunica Kingdom.

Fifteen years ago, a royal girl had been abducted from the castle, her whereabouts unknown ever since.

The kingdom's long history wasn't without its share of ill-fated royals. But she was the only one whose life—though blessed at birth—had gone unrecorded in both growth and end.

"—Filiolé Lugunica."

The daughter of Ford Lugunica, younger brother to the 41st King of Lugunica, Randhal Lugunica. Fifteen years ago, Heinckel had been accused of kidnapping her.

Though the accusation was false—in the end, Heinckel had still committed an unforgivable betrayal against the royal family he'd sworn loyalty to, and against Ford, to whom he owed a great debt.

More than treason against the kingdom, it was a betrayal of the royal family's trust.

"—Bon appétit."

The sinful, grotesque ritual unfolded before his eyes.

From Aldebaran's question, Heinckel had anticipated this. Perhaps even hoped for it. He knew this ritual had triggered countless tragedies. And yet—

Because if it succeeded, he wouldn't have to kill her.

"Thanks for the meal!"

Roy's small back turned as he clapped his hands, concluding the ritual. Heinckel snapped back to reality. He'd been so desperate to look away that he'd literally missed the act, lost in thought.

Each time he committed acts unbecoming of a knight, he mocked the part of himself that still hesitated. As Roy stepped aside with a delighted chuckle, Heinckel moved forward.

And then—

"—Lady Filiolé."

Kneeling, he addressed her.

What reply had he expected? If she'd confirmed her identity, it would mean the ritual failed. If she'd claimed not to know the name, he'd have been tormented all the same.

In the end, Heinckel—who didn't even know his own answer—received this response:

"Who... is that? No, more importantly—"

"— — —"

"—Who am I?"

The reply confirming the ritual's success only wounded and tormented him further.

---

"Filiolé... That's my name, isn't it? It doesn't feel quite right..."

Having had her "memories" eaten, Filiolé's demeanor was that of a completely different person.

The rough speech and mannerisms that had once screamed of poor upbringing were gone, replaced by a grace and gentleness befitting royalty—greatly surprising Heinckel.

Had the tragedy fifteen years ago not occurred, she would have been one of the kingdom's most beloved royals.

"Then again, she might've just croaked from the same disease that got the rest of the royals."

Aldebaran's heartless remark interrupted Heinckel's reflections. But it was true. That mysterious plague that had specifically targeted the royal family would have claimed Filiolé too, had she remained in the castle. The implications of that fact almost dawned on Heinckel—but he shut his eyes, refusing to see.

He was already at his limit, body and soul. With his lifelong wish within reach, each new shocking revelation pushed his heart closer to breaking.

"Is something... troubling you?"

The question came from Filiolé herself, directed at Heinckel as he covered his face.

Despite having no memories and being in a far more precarious position, she still showed concern for others—undeniable proof of Lugunica's royal blood.

At the same time, it confirmed the cruel truth: the moment "Gluttony's" Authority activated, it proved beyond doubt that she was indeed Filiolé Lugunica.

Which meant—

"...So the Royal Selection ends here."

"The hell you mean, old man?"

"Isn't it obvious? The Royal Selection exists to find a worthy 'next' ruler. In other words, it's a contingency for the royal line's extinction. If we now know it wasn't extinct, everyone will flip sides in a heartbeat."

Ironically, it was Priscilla's death, Aldebaran's rampage, and Filiolé's pre-memory-loss words striking Heinckel's weak point that had revealed this truth.

It almost felt like the world itself had orchestrated events to place Filiolé on the throne.

Of course, Heinckel would never crassly suggest Priscilla's death had been part of that design. Ultimately, if Filiolé took the throne, it was what he wanted.

Awaken Luanna. If that could be achieved, everything else would fall into place. That was Heinckel's wish.

So the "Jealous Witch" retreating and Filiolé ascending the throne? Ideal.

"Al, this is the cleanest solution. Got any complaints?"

"...Barely acceptable since you didn't hurt the girl. Damn fine solution, Origin. Just one thing—"

"Yeah?"

"This doesn't seem like your usual thinking. Too clever for you."

"— — —"

While Heinckel convinced himself, Aldebaran and the "Divine Dragon" argued over Filiolé's treatment. At least Heinckel's immediate problem had been shelved—though handling a memory-less Filiolé presented new challenges.

"In that case, it's no problem at all~. Right, Al-sama?"

Yae's sudden appearance at his side made Heinckel gasp. Her mocking sidelong glance suggested she saw right through his unease.

Her attitude wasn't surprising—what was strange was Aldebaran's behavior toward her.

"...Right. I did promise the old man."

"Hah? Promise?"

"Felt—no, Lady Filiolé. We'll handle her."

With eerie detachment, Aldebaran executed his "handling," leaving a bewildered Heinckel behind.

The change in Aldebaran and Yae's relationship, the "Divine Dragon's" feelings toward Filiolé, "Gluttony's" expanded freedom, the truth about Filiolé—their alliance was fragile at best. Heinckel could feel the growing cracks and misalignment.

Amidst it all, he resolved to at least keep his own sanity.

No matter how suffocating the predicament, the group collapsing before his goal was achieved would spell disaster for him.

So—

"—At least I'll stay sane."

As he accepted the black sphere containing Filiolé—now the size of a juggling ball—he swore it.

He swore it.

---

"Back off... if you don't want Lady Filiolé hurt!"

—And yet.

Cornered, Heinckel had reversed that resolution, pinning the grown Filiolé in a chokehold with a sword to her slender neck.

"Felt...!"

The large man called her by another name, his voice thick with anguish.

Flam and Grassis, who had been relentlessly attacking, froze—their gazes a mix of rage, contempt, and likely concern and confusion as they faced Heinckel.

For them, the shock was twofold.

Like their own appearances and disappearances, Filiolé had suddenly appeared in Heinckel's grasp—only for him to call her by another name.

Their eyes brimming with unease and confusion was only natural in such a situation.

"My apologies, but I'd prefer not to resort to violence. You'll cooperate."

As he spoke these deceitful words to Filiolé, Heinckel despised his own hypocrisy.

He had tried. When this sudden battle began, he'd done everything to keep Filiolé out of it. But he'd given up. As always, his efforts bore no fruit.

So he'd shattered the black sphere Aldebaran had entrusted to him, releasing the imprisoned Filiolé—then used her as a hostage to threaten the enemies who appeared.

"Don't move. You don't want her hurt, do you?"

Even as he committed this lowest of acts, his courteous treatment of Filiolé proved a shred of conscience remained.

When he'd been uncertain of her identity, he could dismiss her golden hair and red eyes—the marks of royalty—as mere coincidence. But now that he knew she was Filiolé, he couldn't treat her the same.

If accused of changing his attitude based on the person, he'd admit it. Heinckel had been raised to show unwavering loyalty to the Lugunica royal family.

"—hk!"

His delayed trump card, Filiolé, let out a faint sound as he forced her head up.

Freshly freed from the black sphere, she seemed either dazed or overwhelmed by the sudden turn of events, leaving herself entirely at Heinckel's mercy.

It was frustrating, but the threat worked—his opponents hesitated. Seizing the moment, Heinckel considered securing safety for Roy, who was also trapped in this battlefield, while worrying about Aldebaran's disappearance—

"—FELT!!"

Unlike the large man's earlier strained cry, this voice carried undeniable strength.

The joy of reuniting with a cherished person, the faint anger born of affection, the worry for her safety, and the determination to bring her back—all blended into a single cry that echoed across the battlefield.

"— — —"

At that voice, Filiolé stirred in Heinckel's arms.

Even without memories, even unable to distinguish friend from foe, strong emotions directed at her seemed to get through. Slowly, she blinked her crimson eyes—the proof of her royal blood—and met the gaze of the elderly giant who had called to her.

Her lips parted, forming words—

"—Old Man Rom! Get Gaston and the others back! That thing's coming—!!"

—words that shed all pretense of royal grace, replaced by a fierce, powerful cry.

Like a lion's roar, as if the call of someone important had forcefully restored her supposedly lost "memories."

